  "In Brooks (1968), tasks are easier when the information held and the operation performed use **different types of short-term memory**. For a remembered sentence speaking competes, so pointing is easier. For a remembered shape pointing competes, so speaking is easier. These opposite advantages support some independence between verbal and visual–spatial memory."
